  "raw_response": "{\n  \"title\": \"Four multi-timeframe analysis examples showing HTF context overriding LTF setups\",\n  \"school\": \"Smart Money Concepts (SMC)\",\n  \"asset\": \"EURUSD\",\n  \"timeframe\": \"4H\",\n  \"market_context\": \"The analyst presents four distinct scenarios where higher timeframe structure (daily/4H) informs or overrides lower timeframe (15min/5min/1H) trade decisions: downtrend vs key HTF level, uptrend with room to HTF resistance, consolidation within bullish HTF trend, and pullback requiring LTF confirmation.\",\n  \"key_observation\": \"Price reacting to key HTF market structure levels (e.g., support/resistance zones) often invalidates seemingly valid LTF trend-following trades, and HTF trend direction should dictate bias for LTF entries.\",\n  \"analysis_steps\": [\n    \"Step 1: Identify a clear downtrend on the 15-minute chart and a potential short setup on a pullback, but then check the 4-hour chart to see price approaching a key market structure level (gray zone at ~1.15300–1.15600), raising doubt about the short trade.\",\n    \"Step 2: Observe an uptrend on the 1-hour chart, then zoom to the daily chart to confirm ample space before the next key level, allowing confident long entries on the 1H with HTF-aligned target.\",\n    \"Step 3: Analyze a 5-minute consolidation (red box on GBPUSD chart) appearing trapped in a range, then zoom out to the 1-hour chart to reveal the HTF is bullish, so only long-side breakouts or re-entries are favored; downside breaks are likely failed.\",\n    \"Step 4: For",
